diagnosis_text:
A thorough multi-layer diagnostic pass found no fault in the simple_bgp network. Phase 1 reachability confirmed 0% packet loss and sub-millisecond RTT in both directions between pc1 (195.11.14.2) and pc2 (200.1.1.2). Phase A infrastructure sweep and L2 snapshot found no ACL rules, no duplicate MAC addresses, no IP addressing anomalies, and all six path interfaces UP. Phase B control-plane analysis confirmed both FRR BGP sessions (router1 AS 1 ↔ router2 AS 2) are Established with correct prefix advertisements (195.11.14.0/24 and 200.1.1.0/24), and the TC snapshot found only default `fq_codel` queues — no `tbf` (bandwidth limit), no `netem` (delay/corruption) — on every interface in the data path. Raw `tc qdisc` statistics on all six interfaces confirmed zero drops, zero overlimits, and zero backlog. Phase D pressure sweep found no stress tools, no CPU spikes, and no socket exhaustion on any device. Direct iperf3 throughput tests in both directions yielded ~130–135 Mbps symmetrically with zero TCP retransmits in the pc1→pc2 direction, consistent with normal virtual-routing overhead through two FRR container hops. No helper flagged any device or interface. The user's perception of slowness cannot be confirmed by any objective network measurement in the current lab state: the data plane, control plane, and traffic-shaping layers are all healthy. No corrective action is required on the network side; the user should verify whether the slowness originates in an application or endpoint outside this lab's scope.
